---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Lesson 12: Joystick Control - C00lnerd Tutoring">
    <div style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem;">
        
        <!-- Header -->
        <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem; border-radius: 15px; margin-bottom: 2rem; text-align: center;">
            <h1 style="font-size: 2.5rem; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 1rem 0;">Lesson 12: Joystick Control</h1>
            <p style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 1rem 0; opacity: 0.9;">Two-axis analog input driving the robot arm's base and shoulder servos</p>
            <div style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.2); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 10px; display: inline-block;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: 600;">🕹️ Push the stick a little for slow motion — push it far for fast motion</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Learning Objectives -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🎯 Learning Objectives</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div>
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">By the end of this lesson, you will:</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>Wire a two-axis joystick module and read it with <code>analogRead()</code></li>
                        <li>Measure your joystick's real resting center and build a dead zone around it</li>
                        <li>Make servo speed proportional to how far the stick is pushed</li>
                        <li>Drive two servos at once with non-blocking <code>millis()</code> timing</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Key Concepts:</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>Analog input calibration and center offset</li>
                        <li>Dead zones (ignoring small, noisy inputs)</li>
                        <li>Rate control — deflection maps to step size</li>
                        <li>Incremental servo movement with timed updates</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: Parts Inventory -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📦 Section 1: Parts Inventory</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div style="background: #e6fffa; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">New this lesson</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>1 × two-axis joystick module (KY-023 style, with pushbutton)</li>
                        <li>5 × male-to-female jumper wires for the joystick header</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Carried over from Lesson 11</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>Arduino Uno + USB cable</li>
                        <li>2 × SG90 servos (base + shoulder)</li>
                        <li>Breadboard and jumper wires</li>
                        <li>Optional: piezo buzzer, pushbutton</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Pin Map -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📌 Section 2: Pin Map &amp; Wiring</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                The joystick module has five pins: <strong>GND</strong>, <strong>+5V</strong>, <strong>VRx</strong> (X-axis voltage), <strong>VRy</strong> (Y-axis voltage), and <strong>SW</strong> (the click button inside the stick).
            </p>

            <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Same robot-arm pin map, with the joystick added</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li><strong>Joystick VRx:</strong> A0 (left/right → base servo)</li>
                    <li><strong>Joystick VRy:</strong> A1 (up/down → shoulder servo)</li>
                    <li><strong>Joystick SW:</strong> A3 (to GND when pressed, INPUT_PULLUP)</li>
                    <li><strong>Joystick +5V / GND:</strong> power rail / ground rail</li>
                    <li><strong>Button:</strong> A2 (unchanged from Lesson 11)</li>
                    <li><strong>Buzzer (optional):</strong> pin 8</li>
                    <li><strong>Base servo signal:</strong> pin 9</li>
                    <li><strong>Shoulder servo signal:</strong> pin 10</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #dc2626;">
                <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">⚠️ Power reminder</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    The joystick itself uses almost no current — it can share the Arduino 5V rail safely. The <strong>servos</strong> are still the power-hungry part: if they jitter, feed them from an external 5V supply and keep all grounds tied together.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- How it works -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🕹️ Section 3: How the Joystick Works</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                A two-axis joystick is really <strong>two potentiometers in one package</strong>, plus a pushbutton when you press the stick straight down. You already know how to read a potentiometer — <code>analogRead()</code> returns a value from <strong>0 to 1023</strong>:
            </p>

            <div style="background: #f8fafc; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;">
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li>Stick pushed fully one way → reading near <strong>0</strong></li>
                    <li>Stick resting in the middle → reading near <strong>512</strong></li>
                    <li>Stick pushed fully the other way → reading near <strong>1023</strong></li>
                </ul>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 1rem 0 0 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    "Near 512" is doing a lot of work in that sentence — real joysticks rarely rest at exactly 512, and the value jitters by a few counts even when nobody is touching the stick. Sections 4 and 5 deal with that.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Raw reads -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📟 Section 4: Reading Raw Values</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                First, prove the wiring works. This tiny sketch prints both axes to Serial Monitor — no servos needed yet:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`// Joystick raw value reader
const int joyX = A0;
const int joyY = A1;

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
    int x = analogRead(joyX);
    int y = analogRead(joyY);

    Serial.print("X: ");
    Serial.print(x);
    Serial.print("   Y: ");
    Serial.println(y);

    delay(150);   // slow the stream down so humans can read it
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #f8fafc; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;">
                <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🔬 Try it</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    Upload it, open Serial Monitor, and <strong>don't touch the stick</strong>. Write down the resting X and Y values — that's your joystick's real center. Now push full-left, full-right, full-up, full-down and note which axis changes and in which direction. Every module is a little different.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Center + dead zone -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📍 Section 5: Calibration and the Dead Zone</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Two problems to solve before driving servos. First, your stick's center might be 503 or 521 — not 512. So we <strong>calibrate in setup()</strong>: average a bunch of readings while the stick rests, and remember that as the true center:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`int centerX = 512;
int centerY = 512;
const int deadZone = 25;    // ignore wiggles smaller than this

void calibrateJoystick() {
    long sumX = 0, sumY = 0;
    Serial.println("Calibrating - hands off the stick!");
    for (int i = 0; i < 50; i++) {
        sumX += analogRead(joyX);
        sumY += analogRead(joyY);
        delay(10);
    }
    centerX = sumX / 50;    // true resting center
    centerY = sumY / 50;
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Second, even a resting stick wobbles by ±10 counts or so. The <strong>dead zone</strong> says: if the stick is within 25 counts of center, treat it as centered. Without it, your servos would creep and jitter on their own.
            </p>

            <div style="background: #f8fafc; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;">
                <pre style="margin: 0; color: #4a5568; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.7; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;"><code>{`deflection = reading - center

|deflection| <  deadZone   →   stick counts as centered (do nothing)
|deflection| >= deadZone   →   real input (move, and move faster if far)`}</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Variable speed -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🐢🐇 Section 6: Speed Follows the Stick</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                In Lesson 11, every timed step moved exactly 1°. For joystick control that feels wrong — a small push should creep, a big push should zip. The trick: <strong>keep the timing fixed, and scale the step size with deflection</strong>:
            </p>

            <div style="background: #f8fafc; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <pre style="margin: 0; color: #4a5568; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.7; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;"><code>{`small deflection (just past dead zone)  →  1° per step  →  slow creep
large deflection (stick slammed over)  →  4° per step  →  fast move`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`const int maxStep = 4;   // degrees per step at full deflection

int signOf(int v) {
    return (v > 0) - (v < 0);   // +1, -1, or 0
}

// How many degrees to move this step, given the stick deflection
int stepFor(int deflection) {
    int d = abs(deflection);
    if (d < deadZone) return 0;                    // inside dead zone: hold still
    return map(d, deadZone, 512, 1, maxStep);      // scale 1..4 degrees
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
                <code>signOf(deflection)</code> picks the direction, <code>stepFor(deflection)</code> picks how much. Multiply them and you get a signed step — the rest is the same timed-update pattern from Lesson 11.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Complete sketch -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🧩 Section 7: The Complete Sketch</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Everything together: the joystick drives <strong>X → base</strong> and <strong>Y → shoulder</strong>, clicking the stick sends both servos home to 90°, and all motion uses the non-blocking timed step:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`// ============================================================
// Lesson 12: Joystick Control
// Two-axis joystick driving two SG90 servos:
//   X axis -> base rotation (pin 9)
//   Y axis -> shoulder      (pin 10)
//   SW (stick click) -> both servos return home to 90
// Features: startup calibration, dead zone, speed that scales
// with deflection, non-blocking millis() motion.
// ============================================================

#include <Servo.h>

Servo baseServo;
Servo shoulderServo;

// ---- Pins ----
const int joyX  = A0;
const int joyY  = A1;
const int joySW = A3;    // stick click button (INPUT_PULLUP)

// ---- Servo angles ----
int baseAngle     = 90;
int shoulderAngle = 90;
const int MIN_ANGLE = 20;
const int MAX_ANGLE = 160;

// ---- Joystick calibration ----
int centerX = 512;
int centerY = 512;
const int deadZone = 25;

// ---- Motion timing ----
unsigned long lastStepTime = 0;
const int stepInterval = 15;   // ms between motion steps
const int maxStep = 4;         // degrees per step at full deflection

// ---- Stick button state ----
int lastSWState = HIGH;

int signOf(int v) {
    return (v > 0) - (v < 0);            // +1, -1, or 0
}

// How many degrees to move this step, given stick deflection
int stepFor(int deflection) {
    int d = abs(deflection);
    if (d < deadZone) return 0;                  // ignore tiny wiggles
    return map(d, deadZone, 512, 1, maxStep);    // 1..4 degrees
}

// Measure the true resting center at startup
void calibrateJoystick() {
    long sumX = 0, sumY = 0;
    Serial.println("Calibrating - hands off the stick!");
    for (int i = 0; i < 50; i++) {
        sumX += analogRead(joyX);
        sumY += analogRead(joyY);
        delay(10);
    }
    centerX = sumX / 50;
    centerY = sumY / 50;
    Serial.print("Center X: "); Serial.print(centerX);
    Serial.print("   Center Y: "); Serial.println(centerY);
}

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(9600);
    baseServo.attach(9);
    shoulderServo.attach(10);
    pinMode(joySW, INPUT_PULLUP);
    baseServo.write(baseAngle);
    shoulderServo.write(shoulderAngle);
    calibrateJoystick();
    Serial.println("Joystick control ready. Click stick = HOME.");
}

void loop() {
    // 1. Timed motion step (non-blocking)
    if (millis() - lastStepTime >= stepInterval) {
        lastStepTime = millis();

        int dx = analogRead(joyX) - centerX;   // deflection from center
        int dy = analogRead(joyY) - centerY;

        baseAngle     = constrain(baseAngle     + signOf(dx) * stepFor(dx), MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
        shoulderAngle = constrain(shoulderAngle + signOf(dy) * stepFor(dy), MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);

        baseServo.write(baseAngle);
        shoulderServo.write(shoulderAngle);
    }

    // 2. Stick click -> return home
    int swState = digitalRead(joySW);
    if (swState == LOW && lastSWState == HIGH) {
        baseAngle = 90;
        shoulderAngle = 90;
        Serial.println("HOME");
    }
    lastSWState = swState;
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #ecfdf5; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #059669;">
                <h4 style="color: #065f46; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🔍 Notice the difference from Lesson 11</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    Last lesson, inputs set a <em>target</em> and the servos walked toward it. Here the stick <em>is</em> the motion command — no targets, just "keep stepping this direction at this speed while the stick is held." Both are the same timed-step engine; only the input changes.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Tuning & troubleshooting -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🔧 Section 8: Tuning and Troubleshooting</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Servo moves the wrong way</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">Your joystick may be mounted rotated relative to the arm. Flip the sign: use <code>-dx</code> or <code>-dy</code> in the angle update.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Servo drifts on its own</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">The dead zone is too small or calibration ran while the stick was touched. Raise <code>deadZone</code> to 35 and reset the board.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Too twitchy / too sluggish</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">Tune feel with <code>maxStep</code> (how fast full deflection is) and <code>stepInterval</code> (how often steps happen). Try maxStep 3 and interval 20.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Servo buzzes at the limits</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">You're pushing against <code>MIN_ANGLE</code>/<code>MAX_ANGLE</code> or the mechanical stop. Widen the code limits only if the hardware allows it.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #0ea5e9;">
                <h4 style="color: #0c4a6e; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🛠️ Put It Together — Challenges</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li><strong>Swap the axes:</strong> make Y drive the base and X drive the shoulder. Which feels more natural to a pilot?</li>
                    <li><strong>Add the elbow:</strong> bring back Lesson 11's poses on the A2 button — joystick for manual control, button cycles preset poses.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reserve the click:</strong> right now SW goes home. Future robot-arm jobs for it: gripper open/close, or a mode toggle between manual and automatic.</li>
                    <li><strong>Show your work:</strong> Serial-print base and shoulder angles once a second — you'll want them on a real display in Lesson 13.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div style="text-center; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
                <a href="/programming/quizzes32/lesson12" style="background-color: #7c3aed; color: white; padding: 12px 24px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #6d28d9; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#6d28d9'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#7c3aed'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                    Take Lesson 12 Quiz →
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Navigation -->
        <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-top: 2rem;">
            <div>
                <a href="/programming/lessons32/lesson11" style="background-color: #6b7280; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #4b5563; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#4b5563'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#6b7280'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                    ← Previous: Lesson 11
                </a>
            </div>
